  "language_probability": 1,
  "text": "This pressure cooker cooks faster by making water boil at a higher temperature. Its sealed lid traps steam, so pressure builds above the food. Heating turns some water into steam inside the limited space. The regulator holds the pressure at its designed cooking level. At that higher pressure, water can stay liquid beyond its normal boiling point. The hotter water and steam transfer energy into food more quickly. A locking lid and safety valve help control excess pressure and the cooker must depressurize before opening. So trapped steam raises the boiling temperature and the hotter cooking medium speeds up the meal.",
